# Deliberate overlap between docs pages, with a budget and a reason.
#
# Format:  fileA | fileB | max shared 8-word shingles | why this is correct
#
# Read verify_doc_dupes.py first. An entry here is a CLAIM that two pages must
# talk about the same thing, not a way to silence the gate. The budget is a
# ratchet: growth past it fails, and an entry whose overlap has disappeared
# fails too, so this file cannot quietly rot into a blanket exemption.
#
# Before adding one, try the alternative: give the passage ONE owner and link to
# it. docs/INDEX.md decides which page owns what. Almost every entry below is a
# RULE page and a MECHANISM page naming the same script, flag or failure -- that
# repetition is unavoidable, and each side says something the other does not.

docs/windows-build-invariants.md | docs/windows-builds.md      | 22 | the smoke-gate rule vs the smoke-gate mechanism; both must name the gate, its flags and its floors
docs/windows-build-invariants.md | docs/windows-cross-builds.md| 20 | the same gate rules restated for the arm64 lane, where the floors and skips differ
docs/windows-build-invariants.md | docs/windows-build-lanes.md | 20 | rule ("always commit with --change CMD") vs mechanism (what `docker commit` captures and why)
AGENTS.md                        | docs/windows-builds.md      | 19 | the disk-reclaim guardrails an agent must obey vs the reclaim script's own reference entry
AGENTS.md                        | docs/linux-cross-builds.md  | 18 | the ancestry/digest-pinning rules vs the stage-handoff mechanism they describe
docs/failure-modes.md            | docs/windows-host-setup.md  | 18 | the RDNA4 triage entry and the host FIRST-CHECK box: two entry points, same three actions, on purpose
docs/adopting-in-a-new-project.md| docs/linux-build-basics.md  | 17 | the consumer checklist quotes the `app-runner.sh` surface it is telling you to adopt
docs/windows-build-lanes.md      | docs/windows-build-resources.md | 17 | isolation policy and cache-mount budgets are adjacent decisions and cite the same flags
docs/cross-build-verification.md | docs/linux-cross-builds.md  | 16 | the QEMU/binfmt prerequisite appears as a verification precondition and as a lane step
README.md                        | docs/windows-cross-builds.md | 15 | the README's arm64 summary and the cross-builds status banner both name the cross-target caveat and gate results; the banner owns the detail
docs/failure-modes.md            | docs/windows-cross-builds.md | 17 | the symptom/cause entry and the mechanism paragraph both describe the same AArch64 EH_LABEL root cause; the mechanism page links to failure-modes for the cause
docs/code-quality-tooling.md     | docs/shared-script-libraries.md | 13 | both describe the UV_* wrapper scripts' cwd/PATH contract — one as a code-quality tool, one as a shared library surface; each says something the other does not
